[38;5;231m⏺[39m Before the next question, a hard constraint I need to put on the table, because it limits what "notify" can mean here.

  [1mWith no server, nothing can reach the user while the app is closed.[0m OS-level notifications from a web page require
  either the tab to be alive, or a service worker receiving a [3mpush[0m from a server — and we have no server. So an overdue
  event at 3am cannot ping anyone at 3am. It can only surface when the tab is open, or be computed and shown as a
  backlog the next time the app opens. Any design that promises more than that is lying. I'd rather you know now than
  discover it in review.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how notifications surface, given that ceiling.

  - [1mIn-page inbox only[0m — a bell / panel in the UI, plus a catch-up pass on load for events that fired while away. No
    permission prompt, no browser API, works in every browser, testable with a fake clock. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mIn-page inbox + Web Notifications API[0m — same inbox, plus OS-level toasts when the tab is open but backgrounded.
    Costs a permission prompt (which users deny, and denial is sticky), plus a fallback path you have to build anyway.
    Genuinely useful only for the tab-open-but-hidden case.
  - [1mService worker groundwork[0m — adds a worker now so real push is a smaller step later. Buys nothing today; you asked
    for notifications, not for a deployment target.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-page inbox only, with a catch-up pass on load.[0m The catch-up pass is where the real value is —
  "here's what went overdue while you were gone" — and it's the part that still works when the browser API is denied or
  absent. Adding the Web Notifications layer later is genuinely cheap because it's one more consumer on the same bus;
  that's the payoff for the bus we just agreed on.
